WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.183
Twelve children. One question.
Which fruit do you like best?

2
00:00:09.403 --> 00:00:12.701
You collect every answer,
then draw what the class said.

3
00:00:13.201 --> 00:00:16.219
Before you can draw
anything, you need answers.

4
00:00:16.439 --> 00:00:20.108
So ask every child the same
question, one at a time.

5
00:00:20.328 --> 00:00:25.390
Amina says pineapple. Musa says
banana. Hodan says pineapple.

6
00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:30.115
Ask each child once. All
twelve answers line up in rows.

7
00:00:30.615 --> 00:00:34.190
A list writes every answer
down, one under the other.

8
00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.940
Twelve children, so the
list is twelve lines long.

9
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.782
To count the pineapples you
read down every single line.

10
00:00:42.002 --> 00:00:45.717
A table is shorter. It
writes each answer only once.

11
00:00:45.937 --> 00:00:51.231
Pineapple five, banana four, orange
two, apple one: twelve children.

12
00:00:51.731 --> 00:00:55.493
A block graph turns those
numbers into towers of blocks.

13
00:00:55.713 --> 00:00:59.196
One block means one child,
and nothing else at all.

14
00:00:59.416 --> 00:01:03.456
Five children chose pineapple,
so its tower is five blocks.

15
00:01:03.676 --> 00:01:07.206
Four blocks for banana, two
for orange, one for apple.

16
00:01:07.426 --> 00:01:11.327
Pineapple is the tallest, so
most children chose pineapple.

17
00:01:11.547 --> 00:01:15.355
Apple is the shortest.
Only one child chose apple.

18
00:01:15.855 --> 00:01:19.616
A pictogram shows the same
answers, using little pictures.

19
00:01:19.836 --> 00:01:23.645
One picture stands for one
child, every single time.

20
00:01:23.865 --> 00:01:28.323
The banana row has four pictures,
so four children chose banana.

21
00:01:28.823 --> 00:01:33.235
Now sort. A hoop holds
everything that follows one rule.

22
00:01:33.455 --> 00:01:36.473
Two hoops that cross
over make a Venn diagram.

23
00:01:36.693 --> 00:01:39.805
This hoop is for red.
That hoop is for circles.

24
00:01:40.025 --> 00:01:43.693
A red circle follows both
rules, so it goes in the middle.

25
00:01:43.913 --> 00:01:47.675
A blue triangle follows neither
rule, so it stays outside.

26
00:01:47.895 --> 00:01:51.657
A Carroll diagram does the
same job, using four boxes.

27
00:01:52.157 --> 00:01:56.476
This graph is about pets.
Six have a dog, two a bird.

28
00:01:56.696 --> 00:02:00.550
So more children have a dog
than a bird. Point at it.

29
00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:04.717
Cat and fish are both four,
so their towers match exactly.

30
00:02:04.937 --> 00:02:08.467
The graph never says dogs
are best. It only counts.

31
00:02:08.967 --> 00:02:12.403
You asked everyone once,
and wrote every answer down.

32
00:02:12.623 --> 00:02:16.246
A block graph and a pictogram
show the very same answers.

33
00:02:16.466 --> 00:02:20.460
Hoops and boxes sort. A graph
says what people really said.
